+{"schema_version":1,"plan_id":"bug-002","node_id":"N1","attempt":2,"source":"gate","source_ref":"gate.green ok:false in docs/trace/bug-002/N1/loop.jsonl, head 35122e4b (run 1 392/393: data-1-trace-on-start.test.ts 'DATA-1 · a compile that fails is reported in the console, naming each problem, and no trace.json is served' failed at :138; runs 2 and 3 passed 393/393)","findings":[{"id":"I1","rule_id":"DATA-1","layer":"tests","files":["tests/Uinsure.Policies.Web.Tests/unit/toolchain/data-1-trace-on-start.test.ts","tests/Uinsure.Policies.Web.Tests/unit/toolchain/data-1-first-run.test.ts","src/Uinsure.Policies.Web/vite.config.ts","src/Uinsure.Policies.Web/compiler/index.ts"],"root_cause":"Symptom: in the failing run's log, the captured output has 'trace compile failed: docs/trace/build/F1/activity.jsonl:2 ...' and then 'Local: http://localhost:51476/', but not the loop.jsonl:3 line. So :137 passed and :138 failed. Cause: the test reads the dev server's stdout and stderr into one string (data-1-trace-on-start.test.ts:73-74). startVite resolves as soon as 'Local:' turns up on either stream (:75-90), and the test reads that string only once, at :136. Two pipes have no ordering between them, so a stderr chunk the child wrote before the stdout 'Local:' line can still be read after it. The orchestrator's hypothesis is confirmed, with one correction. Inside the dev server, log.error never races printUrls: the order is fixed. vite.config.ts:42-46 logs every problem synchronously in the 'close' handler and then calls done(). printUrls only runs in a later microtask, through compiled.then (vite.config.ts:50). Vite's logger sends 'error' to console.error, which is stderr, and 'info' (the Local: line) to console.log, which is stdout (node_modules/vite/dist/node/chunks/node.js:14783,14797). The race is in the test process reading the two pipes. The product side is complete and in order. The compiler writes each problem with console.error and sets process.exitCode rather than calling process.exit, so no output is cut off (compiler/index.ts:437-438). The plugin collects stderr until 'close', which fires only after the child's stdio has ended (vite.config.ts:40-42), so `problems` holds both lines. The log also shows the plugin logged the first of the two lines, and the second is written right after it in the same synchronous loop (:44). Missing it can only be a read-timing gap, not a logging gap. The same read-once-at-'Local:' pattern is at data-1-first-run.test.ts:262-265 (the preview branch). It doesn't run at HEAD: traceOnStart never applies under preview (vite.config.ts:35), so the test returns at :256. I've merged it into this finding, on purpose, because it has the same cause. Not the cause: the 'Re-optimizing dependencies because vite config has changed' line. It comes from the shared cacheDir (vite.config.ts:78) seen through the node_modules junction. It adds startup time but doesn't affect which problem lines get captured. Fix direction (product code unchanged, assertions unchanged): after 'Local:' appears, the test should wait, within a deadline, until the captured output names every expected problem (or the server exits), and only then run the same two toContain assertions. The waitFor helper in data-1-first-run.test.ts:90-109 already does this. Apply the same to the dormant preview branch. Don't move the compile problems to stdout, and don't drop or relax the loop.jsonl:3 assertion. Logging all problems in one log.error call would make the race rarer, but it wouldn't remove the cross-pipe ordering gap.","fingerprint":"DATA-1|tests/Uinsure.Policies.Web.Tests/unit/toolchain/data-1-trace-on-start.test.ts|stdout-stderr-read-race-snapshot-at-local","testable":false,"suggested_test":null,"untestable_reason":"The defect is in the test's own observation, not in product behaviour. The dev server writes both problem lines to stderr before 'Local:' goes to stdout on every run (vite.config.ts:42-50). Which order the test process reads the two pipes in is up to the OS pipe reads and the libuv event loop, and no product-facing test can force it. So no test fails reliably on this HEAD. The fix is proved by the existing DATA-1 test, with its assertions unchanged, passing across repeated gate runs."}],"previous_attempts":["attempt 1 · red 6ee2e957 passed its gate · green 35122e4b · gate.green FAIL (flaky, 1 in 3): the problem from loop.jsonl:3 was missing from the captured output. This is the first diagnosis of this cause, so the fingerprint doesn't recur."]}
